    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d193">

                <pb facs="0466" n="426" sameAs="frus:frus1951v05/pg426"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">641.74/11–2651: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Egypt</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Caffery</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Department of State
                        </hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d193-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">priority</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Cairo</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1951-11-26T13:00:00+02:00">November 26,
                            1951—1 p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>767. Deptel 801 Nov 23.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d193-f2"/> “Necessity giving Brit
                    troops rest” formula is not to be taken seriously.</p>
                <p>I agree that immed shut-off effects will not be disastrous. Brit generals are
                    having tough time and we shld be indulgent (Deptel 807 <pb facs="0467" n="427"
                        xml:id="pg427"/> Nov 25).<anchor n="3" xml:id="d193-f3"/> I suggest that we
                    let them play their game for next 2 weeks altho it is bound to have adverse
                    effect on my Serageddin convs and of course King will be particularly angry.</p>
                <p>However, if this state of affairs is allowed to continue “as is” Egypt will
                    eventually fall into lap of my Russian colleague like an overripe plum. Only
                    thing that stands in way of this is our still considerable prestige. Therefore
                    if Brit mil are still playing their game at end of fortnight I reluctantly
                    suggest that there is only one way left for us to protect ourselves and that is
                    the next time after the fortnight they turn off the tap Dept spokesman answer in
                    reply to a planted press question that the US has no responsibility in case of
                    the Brit mil shutting off fuel supplies from Egypt.<anchor n="4"
                        xml:id="d193-f4"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Caffery</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d193-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d193-f1-content">
                <p>Repeated to London, Paris for MacArthur, Rome for Acheson.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d193-f2" n="2" xml:id="d193-f2-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d193-f3" n="3" xml:id="d193-f3-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d193-f4" n="4" xml:id="d193-f4-content">
                <p>In telegram 847 to Cairo of November 30, Webb informed Caffery that as a result
                    of Acheson’s intervention with Eden on the oil cutoff issue, as reported in
                    telegram Secto 87 from Rome, <hi rendition="#italic">infra</hi>, “it will not be
                    necessary consider suggestion re press statement US non-responsibility re oil
                    shutdown.” (641.74/11–2951)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
